<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=nuclear-power-plant">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Energy and power</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nuclear power plant</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1954">1954</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as nuclear power station</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A nuclear power plant is a thermal power station in which the heat source is a nuclear reactor, whose heat makes steam to drive a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steam-turbine">steam turbine</a>. Heat from a reactor was first used to generate electricity on 21 December 1951 at Experimental Breeder Reactor I, which powered four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incandescent-light-bulb">light bulbs</a>. The Obninsk Nuclear Power Plant in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> began operating on 27 June 1954 as the first to supply a power grid, Calder Hall in the United Kingdom opened in 1956, and Shippingport in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> was connected to the grid in 1957.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nuclear-power-plant.jpg" alt="Nuclear power plant" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pilat.Oueb · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Centrale-nucléaire-de-saint-alban-1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Soviet annexation, land and businesses were nationalised and farms collectivised, Russian migrants arrived in the cities and heavy industry, including the Ignalina <b>nuclear power plant</b>, was developed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-lithuania">Republic of Lithuania · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>27 June 1954</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Obninsk, Soviet Union</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Experimental Breeder Reactor I produced electricity from reactor heat on 21 December 1951, powering four light bulbs; Obninsk was the first station to supply a grid.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/steam-turbine">Steam turbine<span>1551</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/chicago-pile-1">Nuclear reactor<span>1942</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=nuclear-power-plant">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
